{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["The sense of <b>and his host, and those that were numbered of them</b> and <b>all that were numbered of the camp of Judah</b> — and likewise all of them — is that they are the words of YHWH to Moses, continuing from \"And YHWH spoke to Moses and to Aaron\" (Numbers 2:1), for He did not count them now a second time. And likewise the sense of <b>These are those that were numbered of the children of Israel</b> (Numbers 2:32) is because it says (there) \"all that were numbered of the camps by their armies.\" And He mentioned all this to make known that from the day of the counting until the camps were arranged and they encamped each by his standard and so they journeyed, not one man of them was missing. And this is a wondrous sign — that for twenty days not a single one of all this great people died. The variation in the name of the father of the prince of the children of Gad is because it is the practice in the sacred language to vary names when the meaning is equivalent, such as Zerah (Numbers 26:13) and Zohar (Genesis 46:10) from the root \"as crimson wool\" (see Ezekiel 27:18). And he was called Deuel — because he knew El — [the same as] Reuel, who set the intention of his heart upon El, as in \"how precious are your thoughts to me, El\" (Psalms 139:17), \"you have schemed against a friend from afar\" (ibid. v. 2). And they would call that man by both names, and therefore the Torah mentions him in both."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "Ramban reads the second Hebrew citation from Psalms 139:2 hyper-literally; the word רֵעִי is read as 'my friend/companion' (= to El), consistent with his etymological argument about Reuel."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:02:14.436091456Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}